text:
Hunger gets harder to bear at the holidays, when everyone else seems to be gathering around a full table. With nearly  48 million Americans  experiencing food insecurity in recent years, including 14 million children, millions of Americans will be facing a Thanksgiving table that has less on it.&nbsp; 

 No single organization can close this gap. It will take food banks, businesses, neighbors and public leaders all showing up. 

 At a time when everything seems to cost more, putting food on the table has become harder than ever. Hunger exists in every county, and  increasingly often  among those who never expected to need food assistance before. More than eight in ten people surveyed by  Feeding America  identified rising costs as the primary driver of food insecurity.&nbsp; 

 The pressure is building for those in need. Recent changes to the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP), the nation’s largest federal nutrition assistance program, have contributed to a significant decline in participation. According to the Food Research Action Center, approximately  5.5 million people  have lost access to SNAP benefits since last year. 

 Food banks and pantries have always been the country’s backstop. But now, like everyone else in this economy, they are being asked to do more with less. Organizations are  bracing demand  rivalling or surpassing experienced during last fall’s government shutdown that created unprecedented disruption to SNAP. Some food banks are  experiencing  demand levels exceeding the COVID-19 pandemic. 

 Getting food to people can be just as challenging as securing it. Many of those served by food banks — including seniors, people with disabilities, or those without reliable transportation — cannot easily get to a distribution site on their own.&nbsp; 

 Tackling this challenge requires everyone contributing what they do best. For  DoorDash , that’s logistics, and closing the gap between food and the people who need it. 

 Through Project DASH, DoorDash transports goods from food banks and pantries to doorsteps. Since 2018, 9 million deliveries of an estimated  150 million meals  across the U.S. and Canada have arrived via DoorDash.&nbsp; 

 This holiday season, we’re  waiving fees  for more than 300 Project DASH partners between Thanksgiving and Christmas, supporting delivery of an estimated 2 million meals. By removing that expense, food bank partners can plan larger holiday distributions with confidence and reach more people. 

 Many of our grocery partners are also doing everything they can to make the holidays more affordable for Americans who are struggling to get by, such as offering discounts for SNAP users to stretch their budgets further. The data is clear: Making it easier to use SNAP benefits can also have a significant impact in the fight against hunger. Since introducing SNAP payments on our platform in 2023, more than  4.5 million  consumers have added their SNAP cards to DoorDash, and nearly a third of those we surveyed said it has helped make them less likely to run out of food. 

 This is only one incremental step in a larger national conversation about hunger. No single company, program, or nonprofit can solve it alone. Food banks understand their neighbors and the needs of their communities, and businesses can bring their resources, technology, and expertise to bear.&nbsp; 

 Hunger is a neighbor-helping-neighbor issue. Americans of every background can agree that no one should go without a meal, especially at the holidays.&nbsp; 

 There is enough food for everyone in America. What’s missing is a way to get it to every table. With everyone at the table, we can build toward that together.   The opinions expressed in Fortune.com commentary pieces are solely the views of their authors and do not necessarily reflect the opinions and beliefs of  Fortune .   This story was originally featured on  Fortune.com
